  <h1>Phoenix Marathon Journal: Day One</h1>
  <div class="postmeta">October 13, 2026 · Journal</div>

  <article>
    <p>Today I decided to run the Phoenix Marathon.</p>

    <p>I have been using AI to look at my training runs, to plan the pace I
    want, and to pressure test what is realistic and what is not. This journal
    is where I will do more of that in public, and write down how it goes.</p>

    <h2>The plan for the plan</h2>
    <p>I am not treating AI as a replacement for the calculator or the other
    way around. The calculator is how I find the pace I want and see the
    splits. AI is how I argue with that pace before the race does. Together
    they give me a much clearer picture than either one does alone.</p>

    <p>If you want the method, I wrote it up in two guides:
    <a href="/use-ai-to-plan-your-marathon-pace/">how to use AI to plan your marathon pace</a>
    and <a href="/analyze-training-runs-with-ai/">what to ask AI about your training runs</a>.</p>

    <h2>What I will report back</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The pace I pick, and the plan behind it, as a live link you can open
      in the calculator.</li>
      <li>What AI said was realistic, what it said was not, and where I
      disagreed.</li>
      <li>What it got wrong. It will get some of it wrong.</li>
      <li>How training actually went against the plan.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>More soon.</p>
  </article>

